xtra lines that cover the points lost.\n\n3. **Construction with \\(k=1\\).**  \n   Start from the \\(k=0\\) construction above and delete one of the horizontal lines, say \\(y=1\\).  The points \\((1,1),\\dots ,(1,n)\\) that were on that line are now uncovered.  They can be covered by a single sunny line of slope \\(\\frac12\\) passing through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,2)\\); this line meets \\(T_n\\) exactly in the points \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,2)\\).  The remaining uncovered points \\((1,3),\\dots ,(1,n)\\) are covered by adding the vertical line \\(x=1\\).  The total number of lines is still \\(n\\) and exactly one of them (the line of slope \\(\\frac12\\)) is sunny.  The same idea works for any choice of the deleted horizontal line, showing that \\(k=1\\) is always attainable.\n\n4. **Construction with \\(k=n-2\\).**  \n   Take the \\(k=0\\) configuration and replace all but two of its lines by distinct sunny lines, each of which passes through a different pair of points of \\(T_n\\).  Because each sunny line can cover at most two points, we need exactly two non‑sunny lines to take care of the remaining \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2-2(n-2)\\) points; a short calculation shows that this number equals \\(n\\), which can be covered by one horizontal and one vertical line.  Hence we obtain a configuration with \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.\n\n5. **Construction with \\(k=n\\).**  \n   Choose \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines, each passing through a distinct point of \\(T_n\\) (for instance, the lines of slopes \\(1,2,\\dots ,n\\) through \\((1,1)\\)).  Because each such line contains at least the point \\((1,1)\\), we first cover \\((1,1)\\) and then use the remaining \\(n-1\\) sunny lines to cover the other points of \\(T_n\\) one‑by‑one.  Since there are exactly \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) points, we can always select the slopes so that every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the chosen lines.  Thus a configuration with all \\(n\\) lines sunny exists.\n\n6. **Restriction on the case \\(k=