ill be added later), and similarly for a horizontal line \\(y=c\\).\n\nWe now perform the following greedy deletion process.\n\n```\nL ← L0\nwhile |L| > n do\n      choose a redundant line L0 ∈ L\n      delete L0 from L\nend while\n```\n\nBecause \\(|\\mathcal L_0|=2n-1>n\\), at each iteration there is at least one redundant line (the diagonal line is never chosen because it is essential). After exactly \\((2n-1)-n=n-1\\) deletions we obtain a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) lines. By construction no deleted line was essential for covering any point, therefore \\(\\mathcal L\\) still covers all points of \\(S_n\\). All its members belong to \\(\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal D\\); consequently every line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) is non‑sunny. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Introducing sunny lines  \n\n#### Lemma 2  \nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) that contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nBegin with the non‑sunny covering family \\(\\mathcal L\\) supplied by Lemma 1.  \nSelect arbitrarily \\(k\\) distinct lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) from \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nFor each \\(i\\) pick a point \\(P_i\\in S_n\\) that lies on \\(L_i\\) (such a point exists because \\(\\mathcal L\\) covers \\(S_n\\)).  \n\nThrough \\(P_i\\) draw a line \\(L_i'\\) whose slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); such a line is sunny and exists in infinitely many copies.  \nReplace \\(L_i\\) by \\(L_i'\\).  \n\nSince \\(P_i\\) already belongs to at least one other line of the family (the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines still cover \\(P_i\\)), the covering condition is not destroyed by the replacement. Repeating this operation for all \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) yields a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that still covers \\(S_n\\) and now contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  At most \\(n-2\\) sunny lines are possible  \n\n#### Lemma 3  \nIn any collection of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points of \\(S_n\\) there are **at least two** non‑sunny lines. Consequently the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n-2\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\(\\mathcal C\\) be a covering family of \\(n\\) distinct lines.  \nDenote by \\(s\\) the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal C\\) and by \\(t=n-s\\) the number of non‑sunny lines.\n\nA sunny line has slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \nConsider a sunny line \\(L\\). Its equation can be written \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(m\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\).  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on \\(L\\) then \\(b=ma+b_0\\) for some constant \\(b_0\\).  \nBecause \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are positive integers and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), the possible values of \\(a\\) are bounded by \\(1\\le a\\le n\\).  \nFor a fixed slope \\(m\\) the map \\(a\\mapsto ma+b_0\\) is injective, hence a sunny line can contain **at most one** point of each vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \nSince there are only \\(n\\) distinct vertical lines intersecting the triangle, a sunny line meets at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  \n\nA sharper bound is obtained by observing that the points of \\(S_n\\) on a given sunny line must satisfy \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \nIf the line contains two distinct points \\((a_1,b_1)\\) and \\((a_2,b_2)\\) with \\(a_1<a_2\\), then \\(b_2-b_1=m(a_2-a_1)\\) with \\(m\\neq 0,-1\\).  \nBecause both points lie under the line \\(a+b=n+1\\), we have  \n\n\\[\na_1+b_1\\le n+1,\\qquad a_2+b_2\\le n+1.\n\\]\n\nSubtracting gives \\((a_2-a_1)+(b_2-b_1)\\le 0\\).  \nSince \\(a_2-a_1>0\\), we must have \\(b_2-b_1<0\\); consequently \\(m<0\\).  \nIf \\(m<-1\\) then \\(|b_2-b_1|>a_2-a_1\\) and the inequality \\((a_2-a_1)+(b_2-b_1)\\le 0\\) forces \\(a_2-a_1\\ge 2\\).  \nIterating this argument shows that a sunny line can contain at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  \n\nThus **any sunny line contains at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(S_n\\).**  \n\nNow count points. By (1) the set \\(S_n\\) has \\(\\binom{n+1}{2}= \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) points.  \nIf \\(\\mathcal C\\) had \\(s=n-1\\) sunny lines and only one non‑sunny line, the maximal number of points that could be covered would be  \n\n\\[\n(s)(n-1)+|S_n\\cap L_{\\text{non‑sunny}}|\n\\le (n-1)(n-1)+|S_n| .\n\\]\n\nBut a single non‑sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\) (it is either horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\)). Hence the total number of points that could be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)(n-1)+n = n^{2}-2n+1+n = n^{2}-n+1 .\n\\]\n\nSince  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}= \\frac{n^{2}+n}{2} > n^{2}-n+1\\qquad\\text{for all }n\\ge 3,\n\\]\n\nthe covering would be impossible.  \n\nA similar calculation shows that with \\(s=n\\) (all lines sunny) the maximal number of covered points is \\(n(n-1)<|S_n|\\).  \n\nTherefore any covering family must contain at least two non‑sunny lines, i.e. \\(t\\ge 2\\). Consequently  \n\n\\[\ns\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\n∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Completion of the proof  \n\nFrom Lemma 2 we know that for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a covering family with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists.  \nLemma 3 shows that no covering family can have more than \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.  \n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]\n\nThis completes the solution. ∎"
