\(i\\le n-2\\) and are therefore also on the corresponding vertical line.  Consequently **every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the \\(n\\) lines**.  This gives a configuration with \\(k=0\\).\n\n#### 2.2  Replacing vertical lines by sunny ones\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  Starting from the base configuration, we shall replace exactly \\(n-2-k\\) of the vertical lines by sunny lines, keeping the total number of lines equal to \\(n\\) and preserving the covering property.\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-2\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y = x - i + 1 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(\\ell_i\\) is \\(1\\), which is sunny (it is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\)).  The line \\(\\ell_i\\) passes through the two points  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,1),\\qquad Q_i=(i+1,2),\n\\]\n\nboth of which belong to \\(T_n\\) because \\(i+1+2\\le n+1\\) for every \\(i\\le n-2\\).\n\nObserve that the vertical line \\(x=i\\) of the base configuration also contains the point \\(P_i\\), while the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) contains \\(P_i\\) as well.  Hence after we delete the vertical line \\(x=i\\) and **add** the sunny line \\(\\ell_i\\), the points \\(P_i\\) and \\(Q_i\\) remain covered (by \\(\\ell_i\\) itself), and all other points that were previously covered by \\(x=i\\) are still covered because they lie on the line \\(x+y=n\\) (which is unchanged).  Therefore the replacement does not destroy the covering property.\n\nNow choose any subset \\(S\\subseteq\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) with \\(|S|=n-2-k\\) and replace, for each \\(i\\in S\\), the vertical line \\(x=i\\) by the sunny line \\(\\ell_i\\).  After performing all replacements we have:\n\n* exactly \\(k\\) vertical lines left (those with indices not in \\(S\\));\n* the two original non‑sunny lines \\(y=1\\) and \\(x+y=n\\);\n* the \\(n-2-k\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_i\\) with \\(i\\in S\\).\n\nThus the total number of lines is  \n\n\\[\nk\\;(\\text{vertical})\\