e listed lines.  This yields a configuration with \\(k=0\\).\n\n#### 2.2  Replacing vertical lines by sunny ones\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-2\\) set  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y = x-i+1 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(\\ell_i\\) is \\(1\\); therefore \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.  \n\\(\\ell_i\\) passes through the two points  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,1),\\qquad Q_i=(i+1,2),\n\\]\n\nboth belonging to \\(T_n\\) because \\(i+1+2\\le n+1\\).\n\nObserve that the vertical line \\(x=i\\) of the base configuration also contains \\(P_i\\), while the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) contains \\(P_i\\) as well.  Consequently, if we **delete** the vertical line \\(x=i\\) and **add** the sunny line \\(\\ell_i\\), the points \\(P_i\\) and \\(Q_i\\) remain covered (by \\(\\ell_i\\)), and every other point that was on \\(x=i\\) is still covered by the unchanged diagonal line \\(x+y=n\\).  Hence the covering property is preserved.\n\nNow fix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nChoose any subset \\(S\\subseteq\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) of size \\(|S|=n-2-k\\) and perform the replacement described above for each \\(i\\in S\\).\n\nAfter all replacements we have:\n\n* the \\(k\\) vertical lines \\(x=i\\) with \\(i\\notin S\\) (non\u2011sunny);\n* the \\(n-2-k\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_i\\) with \\(i\\in S\\);\n* the two unchanged non\u2011sunny lines \\(y=1\\) and \\(x+y=n\\).\n\nThus the total number of lines is  \n\n\\[\nk+(n-2-k)+2=n,\n\\]\n\nand the number of sunny lines is exactly \\(n-2-k = k_{\\text{desired}}\\) after relabelling the parameter; i.e. we have produced a configuration with **exactly the prescribed** \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nSince the choice of \\(S\\) is arbitrary, we obtain a valid configuration for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Final answer\n\nFrom (1) we know that \\(k\\) cannot exceed \\(n-2\\).  \nThe construction of \u00a72 shows that every intege