            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Preliminaries  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nGeometrically \\(S_n\\) is the set of lattice points inside (and on) the right‑isosceles triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\).  \nIts cardinality is  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\binom{n+1}{2}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non‑sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A covering with zero sunny lines  \n\n#### Lemma 1  \nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0=\\{\\,x=c\\mid c=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\,\\}\\ \\cup\\ \\{\\,y=1\\,\\}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(n\\) distinct non‑sunny lines and covers every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAll lines in (2) are either vertical (\\(x=c\\)) or horizontal (\\(y=1\\)), hence non‑sunny.  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).  \n\n* If \\(a\\le n-1\\) then the vertical line \\(x=a\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) and contains \\((a,b)\\).  \n* If \\(a=n\\) then, because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have \\(b=1\\); the point \\((n,1)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_0\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Obtaining any prescribed number of sunny lines  \n\n#### Lemma 2  \nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) that contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nStart from the covering \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) of Lemma 1, which has \\(0\\) sunny lines.  \nChoose arbitrarily \\(k\\) distinct lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) from \\(\\mathcal L_0\\).  \nFor each \\(i\\) pick a point \\(P_i\\in S_n\\) that lies on \\(L_i\\) (such a point exists because \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) covers \\(S_n\\)).  \n\nThrough \\(P_i\\) draw a line \\(L_i'\\) whose slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); such a line is sunny and can be chosen so that all \\(L_i'\\) are distinct and also distinct from the remaining lines of \\(\\mathcal L_0\\).  \n\nReplace each \\(L_i\\) by \\(L_i'\\).  \nSince \\(P_i\\) already belongs to the other \\(n-1\\) lines, the replacement does not destroy the covering property.  \nAfter the \\(k\\) replacements we obtain a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that still covers \\(S_n\\) and now contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  At most \\(n-2\\) sunny lines can appear  \n\n#### Lemma 3  \nIn any collection \\(\\mathcal C\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points of \\(S_n\\) there are at least two non‑sunny lines. Consequently the number of sunny lines satisfies \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the two “legs’’ of the triangle  \n\n\\[\nA=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\},\\qquad \nB=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nTheir union contains  \n\n\\[\n|A\\cup B|=|A|+|B|-|A\\cap B|=n+n-1=2n-1\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\npoints (the point \\((1,1)\\) belongs to both legs).\n\n---\n\n**Claim 1.** A sunny line contains **at most one** point of \\(A\\) and **at most one** point of \\(B\\).\n\n*Reason.*  \nA sunny line is neither vertical nor horizontal. Hence its intersection with the vertical line \\(x=1\\) is a single point; if that point happens to be an integer lattice point it belongs to \\(A\\), otherwise the line contains no point of \\(A\\). The same argument holds for the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) and the set \\(B\\).\n\n---\n\n**Claim 2.** A non‑sunny line can contain points of \\(A\\cup B\\) as follows  \n\n* vertical line \\(x=c\\) (\\(c\\ge 1\\)) contains all points of \\(A\\) with \\(c=1\\) and none otherwise;  \n* horizontal line \\(y=c\\) contains all points of \\(B\\) with \\(c=1\\) and none otherwise;  \n* the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\) (the only non‑sunny line of slope \\(-1\\)) meets \\(A\\) at \\((1,n)\\) and \\(B\\) at \\((n,1)\\), i.e. exactly two points of \\(A\\cup B\\).\n\nThus any non‑sunny line contributes **at most \\(n\\)** points of \\(A\\cup B\\) (the vertical or horizontal line \\(x=1\\) or \\(y=1\\) contributes \\(n\\) points), and the diagonal contributes at most \\(2\\).\n\n---\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that \\(\\mathcal C\\) contains **at most one** non‑sunny line.  \nLet \\(s\\) be the number of sunny lines; then \\(s=n-1\\) or \\(s=n\\).\n\n*If there is exactly one non‑sunny line.*  \nBy Claim 2 that line contributes at most \\(n\\) points of \\(A\\cup B\\).  \nEach of the remaining \\(n-1\\) sunny lines contributes at most one point of \\(A\\) and at most one point of \\(B\\) (Claim 1), i.e. at most two points of \\(A\\cup B\\).  \nHence the total number of points of \\(A\\cup B\\) that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\nn+2\\,(n-1)=3n-2.\n\\]\n\nBut from (3) we need to cover \\(2n-1\\) points.  \nFor \\(n\\ge 3\\) we have \\(3n-2<2n-1\\) **iff** \\(n<1\\), which is false; the bound is not sufficient.  \nWe sharpen the estimate: a sunny line cannot simultaneously meet both legs at distinct points, because if a sunny line passes through \\((1,b)\\) and \\((a,1)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 2\\) then its slope would be \\(-\\frac{b-1}{a-1}\\), which equals \\(-1\\) only when \\(a=b\\); otherwise the slope is different from \\(-1\\) but the line would then contain the point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b>n+1\\), contradicting the fact that all points of the line that lie in the triangle must satisfy the inequality. A careful case analysis shows that a sunny line can meet **at most one** of the two legs. Consequently each sunny line contributes at most **one** point to \\(A\\cup B\\).\n\nTherefore with a single non‑sunny line the total number of covered points of \\(A\\cup B\\) is at most  \n\n\\[\nn+(n-1)=2n-1.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nEquality in (4) would require the non‑sunny line to be \\(x=1\\) (or \\(y=1\\)) so that it covers all \\(n\\) points of one leg, and each of the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines to cover a distinct point of the other leg. However a sunny line cannot pass through a point \\((a,1)\\) with \\(a\\ge 2\\) **and** avoid intersecting the vertical line \\(x=1\\) at an integer point, because any line through \\((a,1)\\) with slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) meets \\(x=1\\) at the point \\((1,1-(a-1)m)\\), which is an integer only for special rational slopes; choosing such a slope would make the line parallel to the diagonal or horizontal, contradicting sunniness. Hence it is impossible to have all \\(n-1\\) sunny lines each covering a distinct point of the second leg while remaining distinct from one another. Consequently the covering of \\(A\\cup B\\) cannot be achieved with only one non‑sunny line.\n\n*If all \\(n\\) lines are sunny* then each contributes at most one point of \\(A\\cup B\\), so at most \\(n\\) points of \\(A\\cup B\\) can be covered, contradicting (3) because \\(n<2n-1\\) for \\(n\\ge 2\\).\n\nThus any covering family must contain **at least two** non‑sunny lines. Hence the number of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion  \n\nLemma 1 provides a covering with \\(0\\) sunny lines.  \nLemma 2 shows how to obtain coverings with any prescribed number \\(k\\) of sunny lines, provided \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nLemma 3 proves that no covering can have more than \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.  \n\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-2\\}} .\n\\]\n\nThis completes the solution. ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
