ing \\(S_n\\) that contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nStart from the covering \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) of Lemma 1, which has \\(0\\) sunny lines.  \nChoose arbitrarily \\(k\\) distinct lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) from \\(\\mathcal L_0\\).  \nFor each \\(i\\) pick a point \\(P_i\\in S_n\\) that lies on \\(L_i\\) (such a point exists because \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) covers \\(S_n\\)).  \n\nThrough \\(P_i\\) draw a line \\(L_i'\\) whose slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); such a line is sunny and can be chosen so that all \\(L_i'\\) are distinct and also distinct from the remaining lines of \\(\\mathcal L_0\\).  \n\nReplace each \\(L_i\\) by \\(L_i'\\).  \nSince \\(P_i\\) already belongs to the other \\(n-1\\) lines, the replacement does not destroy the covering property.  \nAfter the \\(k\\) replacements we obtain a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that still covers \\(S_n\\) and now contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  At most \\(n-2\\) sunny lines can appear  \n\n#### Lemma 3  \nIn any collection \\(\\mathcal C\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points of \\(S_n\\) there are at least two non‑sunny lines. Consequently the number of sunny lines satisfies \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the two “legs’’ of the triangle  \n\n\\[\nA=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\},\\qquad \nB=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nTheir union contains  \n\n\\[\n|A\\cup B|=|A|+|B|-|A\\cap B|=n+n-1=2n-1\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\npoints (the point \\((1,1)\\) belongs to both legs).\n\n---\n\n**Claim 1.** A sunny line contains **at most one** point of \\(A\\) and **at most one** point of \\(B\\).\n\n*Reason.*  \nA sunny line is neither vertical nor horizontal. Hence its intersection with the vertical line \\(x=1\\) is a single point; if that point happens to be an integer lattice point it belongs to \\(A\\), otherwise the line contains no point of \\(A\\). The same argument holds