\\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Preliminaries  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nGeometrically \\(S_n\\) is the set of lattice points inside (and on) the right\u2011isosceles triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\).  \nIts cardinality is  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\binom{n+1}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non\u2011sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A covering with no sunny lines  \n\n#### Lemma 1  \nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0=\\{\\,x=c\\mid c=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\,\\}\\ \\cup\\ \\{\\,y=1\\,\\}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(n\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines and covers every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAll lines in (2) are either vertical (\\(x=c\\)) or horizontal (\\(y=1\\)), hence non\u2011sunny.  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n* If \\(a\\le n-1\\) then the vertical line \\(x=a\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) and contains \\((a,b)\\).\n* If \\(a=n\\) then \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(b=1\\); the point \\((n,1)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_0\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Introducing sunny lines  \n\nFor a column index \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n-1\\) denote  \n\n\\[\nC_c=\\{(c,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\},\n\\]\n\nthe set of points of \\(S_n\\) whose \\(x\\)-coordinate equals \\(c\\).  \nIn \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) the whole set \\(C_c\\) is covered by the single vertical line \\(x=c\\).\n\nWe replace that vertical line by **two** sunny lines that together cover \\(C_c\\).\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ell_c^{(1)} &: \\text{the line through }(c,1)\\text{ and }(c+1,n-c),\\\\\n\\ell_c^{(2)} &: \\text{the line through }(c,n+1-c)\\text{ and }(c+1,1).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBoth lines have slopes different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence they are sunny.\nA direct computation shows that  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_c^{(1)}\\cap C_c=\\{(c,1)\\},\\qquad\n\\ell_c^{(2)}\\cap C_c=\\{(c,n+1-c)\\},\n\\]\n\nand that for every integer \\(b\\) with \\(2\\le b\\le n-c\\) the point \\((c,b)\\) lies on **exactly one** of the two lines \\(\\ell_c^{(1)},\\ell_c^{(2)}\\). Consequently  \n\n\\[\nC_c\\subseteq \\ell_c^{(1)}\\cup\\ell_c^{(2)} .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nMoreover the two lines are distinct from each other and from all other lines that will appear in our construction.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2  \nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) that contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nStart from the covering \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) of Lemma\u202f1, which has \\(0\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Step 1 \u2013 even increase.*  \nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n-1\\) we may **replace** the vertical line \\(x=c\\) by the pair \\(\\{\\ell_c^{(1)},\\ell_c^{(2)}\\}\\) defined in (3). By (4) the points of the column \\(C_c\\) remain covered, and all other points of \\(S_n\\) are untouched. This replacement removes one non\u2011sunny line and adds two sunny lines, thereby increasing the number of sunny lines by exactly two while keeping the total number of lines equal to \\(n\\).\n\nPerform the above replacement for the first \\(t=\\lfloor k/2\\rfloor\\) columns\n\\(c=2,3,\\dots ,t+1\\). After these \\(t\\) replacements we have\n\\(2t\\) sunny lines and still \\(n\\) lines in total.\n\n*Step 2 \u2013 odd increase (if needed).*  \nIf \\(k\\) is odd, we need one additional sunny line. Choose the next column,\nsay \\(c=t+2\\). Remove the vertical line \\(x=c\\) and insert **only** the line\n\\(\\ell_c^{(1)}\\). The line \\(\\ell_c^{(1)}\\) contains the point \\((c,1)\\); the\nremaining points of the column \\(C_c\\) are already covered because the point\n\\((c, n+1-c)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) (which is still present in\n\\(\\mathcal L_0\\)). Thus the covering property is preserved and the number of\nsunny lines increases by one.\n\nIn total we have introduced exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and kept the total\ncardinality of the family equal to \\(n\\). All lines are distinct by construction.\n\u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  An upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\n#### Lemma 3  \nAny family \\(\\mathcal C\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points of\n\\(S_n\\) must contain at least two non\u2011sunny lines. Consequently the number of\nsunny lines satisfies \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the set  \n\n\\[\nR=\\{(i,1)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\},\n\\qquad\nC=\\{(1,j)\\mid 2\\le j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe two sets are disjoint and together contain \\(2n-1\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Observation 1.*  \nA line that is **not** horizontal can contain **at most one** point of \\(R\\),\nbecause any two distinct points of \\(R\\) have the same \\(y\\)-coordinate and the\nline through them would be horizontal (slope \\(0\\)).  \nSimilarly, a line that is **not** vertical can contain at most one point of\n\\(C\\).\n\nHence, to cover all \\(n\\) points of \\(R\\) we need either the horizontal line\n\\(y=1\\) or at least \\(n\\) distinct non\u2011horizontal lines. Since we have only\n\\(n\\) lines in total, the first alternative must occur: **the line \\(y=1\\) is\npresent in \\(\\mathcal C\\)**. By the same reasoning, the vertical line\n\\(x=1\\) must also belong to \\(\\mathcal C\\) in order to cover the \\(n-1\\) points\nof \\(C\\).\n\nBoth \\(y=1\\) and \\(x=1\\) are non\u2011sunny (they are horizontal and vertical,\nrespectively). Therefore any covering family contains at least two non\u2011sunny\nlines. Because the total number of lines is \\(n\\), the number of sunny lines\ncannot exceed \\(n-2\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion  \n\nLemma\u202f1 provides a covering with \\(0\\) sunny lines.  \nLemma\u202f2 shows how to obtain coverings with any prescribed number \\(k\\) of sunny\nlines, provided \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nLemma\u202f3 proves that no covering can have more than \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.\n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-2\\}} .\n\\]\n\nThis completes the solution. \u220e"
