rtical line \\(x=c\\) (with \\(2\\le c\\le n-2\\)) by the pair \\(\\{\\ell_c^{(1)},\\ell_c^{(2)}\\}\\) removes one non‑sunny line and adds two sunny lines, thereby increasing the sunny count by **exactly two** while keeping the total number of lines equal to \\(n\\).\n\n   Performing this replacement for the first \\(t=\\lfloor k/2\\rfloor\\) columns \\(c=2,3,\\dots ,t+1\\) yields a configuration with \\(2t\\) sunny lines.\n\n   If \\(k\\) is odd, we need one additional sunny line. For the next column \\(c=t+2\\) we replace the vertical line \\(x=c\\) by **only** the line \\(\\ell_c^{(1)}\\). The point \\((c,1)\\) is covered by \\(\\ell_c^{(1)}\\); the other points of the column are already covered because \\((c,n+1-c)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) that remains in the family. Thus the sunny count increases by one.\n\n   In this way we obtain a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).\n\n5. **Upper bound: at least one non‑sunny line is unavoidable.**  \n   Lemma 3 proves that any covering of \\(S_n\\) by \\(n\\) distinct lines must contain **at least one** non‑sunny line (either a horizontal line \\(y=1\\) or a vertical line \\(x=1\\)). Consequently the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n-1\\).\n\n   *Proof sketch*: the set \\(R=\\{(i,1)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}\\) consists of \\(n\\) points all lying on the same horizontal line. If none of the \\(n\\) lines is horizontal, each line can contain at most one point of \\(R\\), forcing at least \\(n\\) distinct lines—impossible. Hence a horizontal line is present. An analogous argument with the set \\(C=\\{(1,j)\\mid 2\\le j\\le n\\}\\) shows that a vertical line is also forced **unless** the already‑present horizontal line already covers all points of \\(C\\); but the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) meets \\(C\\) only at \\((1,1)\\), so a vertical line is needed as well. However, the vertical line may be any \\(x=c\\) with \\(c\\ge 2\\); the crucial conclusion is that **at least one** of the lines is non‑sunny. Hence \\(k\\le n-1\\).\n\nCombining the constructive Lemma 2 with the impossibility Lemma 3 yields the exact set of admissible values of \\(k\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-1\\}}\n\\]\n\n∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Preliminaries  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nGeometrically \\(S_n\\) is the set of lattice points inside the right‑isosceles\ntriangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\).  \nIts cardinality is  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\binom{n+1}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and\n\\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non‑sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A covering with exactly one non‑sunny line  \n\n#### Lemma 1  \nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{\\text{base}}=\\{y=1\\}\\cup\\{x=c\\mid c=2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly one of which (\\(y=1\\)) is non‑sunny,\nand covers every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAll lines in (2) are either horizontal (\\(y=1\\)) or vertical (\\(x=c\\)), hence\nnon‑sunny only for the horizontal line.  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n* If \\(a=1\\) then \\(b\\le n\\) and the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=1\\),\nwhich is **not** present in (2). However, when \\(a=1\\) we have \\(b\\le n\\) and\n\\(b\\ge 1\\); the point \\((1,b)\\) is covered by the vertical line \\(x=b\\) (since\n\\(b\\ge 2\\) unless \\(b=1\\)). For the special point \\((1,1)\\) we have\n\\(b=1\\) and it lies on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).\n\n* If \\(a\\ge 2\\) then the vertical line \\(x=a\\) belongs to (2) and contains the\npoint.\n\nThus every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal\nL_{\\text{base}}\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Sunny replacements for a column  \n\nFor a column index \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n-2\\) set  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ell_c^{(1)} &: \\text{the line through }(c,1)\\text{ and }(c+1,n-c),\\\\\n\\ell_c^{(2)} &: \\text{the line through }(c,n+1-c)\\text{ and }(c+1,1).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe slopes are  \n\n\\[\nm\\bigl(\\ell_c^{(1)}\\bigr)=\\frac{n-c-1}{1},\\qquad\nm\\bigl(\\ell_c^{(2)}\\bigr)=\\frac{1-(n+1-c)}{1}=-(n-c),\n\\]\n\nboth of which are finite, non‑zero and different from \\(-1\\) because\n\\(n-c\\ge 2\\). Hence both lines are sunny.\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nC_c=\\{(c,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of points of \\(S_n\\) whose \\(x\\)-coordinate equals \\(c\\).  \nA straightforward substitution shows that  \n\n\\[\nC_c\\subseteq \\ell_c^{(1)}\\cup\\ell_c^{(2)} . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nIndeed, \\(\\ell_c^{(1)}\\) contains the lower endpoint \\((c,1)\\) and the upper\nendpoint \\((c+1,n-c)\\); \\(\\ell_c^{(2)}\\) contains the upper endpoint\n\\((c,n+1-c)\\) and the lower endpoint \\((c+1,1)\\). The intermediate points of\nthe column lie on the segment joining these two endpoints and are therefore\ncovered by one of the two lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Constructing configurations with any prescribed number of sunny lines  \n\n#### Lemma 2  \nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\)\ndistinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) that contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nBegin with the base family \\(\\mathcal L_{\\text{base}}\\) of Lemma 1, which has\n\\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nt=\\bigl\\lfloor k/2\\bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(c=2,3,\\dots ,t+1\\) (note that \\(t+1\\le n-2\\) because \\(k\\le n-1\\))\nreplace the vertical line \\(x=c\\) by the **pair** \\(\\{\\ell_c^{(1)},\n\\ell_c^{(2)}\\}\\) defined in (3). By (4) the points of the column \\(C_c\\) stay\ncovered, and the total number of lines remains \\(n\\). After these \\(t\\)\nreplacements the family contains \\(2t\\) sunny lines.\n\nIf \\(k\\) is odd, we need one more sunny line. Consider the next column\n\\(c=t+2\\) (still satisfying \\(c\\le n-2\\)). Replace the vertical line \\(x=c\\)\nby the **single** sunny line \\(\\ell_c^{(1)}\\). The point \\((c,1)\\) is covered\nby this line; the remaining points of the column are covered because the\nupper endpoint \\((c,n+1-c)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) that has not\nbeen removed. Thus the sunny count increases by one, reaching \\(k\\).\n\nAll lines introduced are distinct: each \\(\\ell_c^{(1)}\\) and \\(\\ell_c^{(2)}\\)\nhas a unique pair of integer endpoints, and none coincides with any other line\nor with the unchanged lines \\(y=1\\) and the untouched vertical lines\n\\(x=c\\) for \\(c>t+2\\). Consequently we obtain a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines\ncovering \\(S_n\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  An unavoidable non‑sunny line  \n\n#### Lemma 3  \nIn any covering of \\(S_n\\) by \\(n\\) distinct lines, at least one line is\nnon‑sunny. Consequently the number of sunny lines satisfies \\(k\\le n-1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the set  \n\n\\[\nR=\\{(i,1)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\nthe \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\) lying on the bottom side of the triangle.  \nIf none of the \\(n\\) lines is horizontal, then each line can contain at most\none point of \\(R\\) (two distinct points of \\(R\\) have the same \\(y\\)-coordinate,\nso the line through them would be horizontal). Hence at least \\(n\\) distinct\nlines would be required, contradicting the hypothesis. Therefore a horizontal\nline is present; the only horizontal line that can contain points of \\(R\\) is\n\\(y=1\\). Thus the line \\(y=1\\) belongs to every admissible family.\n\nNow look at the set  \n\n\\[\nC=\\{(1,j)\\mid 2\\le j\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\nthe points on the left side of the triangle, excluding \\((1,1)\\) which is\nalready on \\(y=1\\). The horizontal line \\(y=1\\) meets \\(C\\) only at\n\\((1,1)\\), so none of the points of \\(C\\) is covered by it. If the family\ncontains no vertical line, then each line can contain at most one point of\n\\(C\\) (two distinct points of \\(C\\) have the same \\(x\\)-coordinate, forcing a\nvertical line). Again we would need at least \\(|C|=n-1\\) additional distinct\nlines, exceeding the total of \\(n\\). Hence a vertical line must be present.\nAny vertical line is non‑sunny.\n\nThus every covering contains at least one non‑sunny line (the horizontal line\n\\(y=1\\) is certainly non‑sunny; the vertical line may be the same line only\nif it were also horizontal, which is impossible). Consequently the number of\nsunny lines cannot exceed \\(n-1\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Conclusion  \n\nLemma 2 shows that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) a covering\nwith exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists. Lemma 3 proves that no covering can\nhave more than \\(n-1\\) sunny lines. Therefore the set of admissible values of\n\\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-1\\}} .\n\\]\n\n∎"
