            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Preliminaries  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nGeometrically \\(S_n\\) is the set of lattice points inside the right‑isosceles\ntriangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\).  \nIts cardinality is  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\binom{n+1}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and\n\\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non‑sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A covering with exactly one non‑sunny line  \n\n#### Lemma 1  \nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{\\text{base}}=\\{y=1\\}\\cup\\{x=c\\mid c=2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly one of which (\\(y=1\\)) is non‑sunny,\nand covers every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAll lines in (2) are either horizontal (\\(y=1\\)) or vertical (\\(x=c\\)), hence\nnon‑sunny only for the horizontal line.  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n* If \\(a=1\\) then \\(b\\le n\\) and the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=1\\),\nwhich is **not** present in (2). However, when \\(a=1\\) we have \\(b\\le n\\) and\n\\(b\\ge 1\\); the point \\((1,b