 with the base family \\(\\mathcal L_{\\text{base}}\\) of Lemma 1, which has\n\\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nt=\\bigl\\lfloor k/2\\bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(c=2,3,\\dots ,t+1\\) (note that \\(t+1\\le n-2\\) because \\(k\\le n-1\\))\nreplace the vertical line \\(x=c\\) by the **pair** \\(\\{\\ell_c^{(1)},\n\\ell_c^{(2)}\\}\\) defined in (3). By (4) the points of the column \\(C_c\\) stay\ncovered, and the total number of lines remains \\(n\\). After these \\(t\\)\nreplacements the family contains \\(2t\\) sunny lines.\n\nIf \\(k\\) is odd, we need one more sunny line. Consider the next column\n\\(c=t+2\\) (still satisfying \\(c\\le n-2\\)). Replace the vertical line \\(x=c\\)\nby the **single** sunny line \\(\\ell_c^{(1)}\\). The point \\((c,1)\\) is covered\nby this line; the remaining points of the column are covered because the\nupper endpoint \\((c,n+1-c)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) that has not\nbeen removed. Thus the sunny count increases by one, reaching \\(k\\).\n\nAll lines introduced are distinct: each \\(\\ell_c^{(1)}\\) and \\(\\ell_c^{(2)}\\)\nhas a unique pair of integer endpoints, and none coincides with any other line\nor with the unchanged lines \\(y=1\\) and the untouched vertical lines\n\\(x=c\\) for \\(c>t+2\\). Consequently we obtain a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines\ncovering \\(S_n\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  An unavoidable non‑sunny line  \n\n#### Lemma 3  \nIn any covering of \\(S_n\\) by \\(n\\) distinct lines, at least one line is\nnon‑sunny. Consequently the number of sunny lines satisfies \\(k\\le n-1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the set  \n\n\\[\nR=\\{(i,1)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\nthe \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\) lying on the bottom side of the triangle.  \nIf none of the \\(n\\) lines is horizontal, then each line can contain at most\none point of \\(R\\) (two distinct points of \\(R\\) have the same \\(y\