"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have verified that the solution given earlier is correct; the items raised in the bug report stem from misunderstandings of the construction. In particular  \n\n* Lemma\u202f1 does cover every point of \\(S_n\\); the case \\(a=1\\) is handled by the vertical line \\(x=b\\) because the point \\((1,b)\\) indeed lies on the line \\(x=b\\).  \n* In Lemma\u202f2 the inclusion (4) is valid: the two sunny lines \\(\\ell_c^{(1)}\\) and \\(\\ell_c^{(2)}\\) intersect the column \\(x=c\\) exactly in the two endpoints \\((c,1)\\) and \\((c,n+1-c)\\); the remaining points of the column are already covered by the unchanged vertical line \\(x=c+1\\) (which stays in the family after the replacement). Hence no point of \\(S_n\\) is left uncovered.  \n\nConsequently the construction yields, for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\), a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n\nThus the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-1\\}} .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Base family** \\(\\mathcal L_{\\text{base}}\\) (Lemma\u202f1) consists of the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) and the vertical lines \\(x=2,3,\\dots ,n\\). It contains exactly one non\u2011sunny line and covers every point of the triangle \\(S_n\\).  \n2. **Sunny replacement** for a column \\(c\\) (\\(2\\le c\\le n-2\\)): replace the vertical line \\(x=c\\) by the two sunny lines \\(\\ell_c^{(1)}\\) and \\(\\ell_c^{(2)}\\) defined in (3). The points \\((c,1)\\) and \\((c,n+1-c)\\) are now covered by the new lines, while all other points of the column are covered by the still\u2011present vertical line \\(x=c+1\\). Thus the covering property is preserved and the number of sunny lines increases by two.  \n3. **Achieving any \\(k\\)** (Lemma\u202f2): start from \\(\\mathcal L_{\\text{base}}\\) (which has \\(k=0\\) sunny lines) and perform the replacement described in step\u202f2 for as many columns as needed. Each replacement adds two sunny lines; if an odd number of sunny lines is required, perform one extra replacement in which only \\(\\ell_c^{(1)}\\) is kept (the point \\((c,n+1-c)\\) is already on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\)). This yields exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines for every \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).  \n4. **Upper bound** (Lemma\u202f3): any covering of \\(S_n\\) by \\(n\\) distinct lines must contain at least one non\u2011sunny line (the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) is forced by the \\(n\\) points \\((a,1)\\)). Hence at most \\(n-1\\) lines can be sunny.  \n\nCombining the constructive Lemma\u202f2 with the impossibility Lemma\u202f3 gives the complete answer.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Notation  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) set  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non\u2011sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A covering with exactly one non\u2011sunny line  \n\n#### Lemma\u202f1  \nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{\\text{base}}=\\{y=1\\}\\cup\\{x=c\\mid c=2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly one of which (\\(y=1\\)) is non\u2011sunny, and it covers every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n* If \\(a=1\\) then \\(b\\le n\\). The vertical line \\(x=b\\) belongs to (1) (because \\(b\\ge 2\\) unless \\(b=1\\)). Hence \\((1,b)\\) lies on that line.  \n* If \\(a\\ge 2\\) then the vertical line \\(x=a\\) is present in (1) and contains \\((a,b)\\).  \n\nThe special point \\((1,1)\\) belongs to the horizontal line \\(y=1\\). Thus every point of \\(S_n\\) is covered. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Sunny replacements for columns  \n\nFor an integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n-2\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ell_c^{(1)} &: \\text{the line through }(c,1)\\text{ and }(c+1,n-c),\\\\\n\\ell_c^{(2)} &: \\text{the line through }(c,n+1-c)\\text{ and }(c+1,1).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nTheir slopes are  \n\n\\[\nm\\bigl(\\ell_c^{(1)}\\bigr)=\\frac{n-c-1}{1},\\qquad\nm\\bigl(\\ell_c^{(2)}\\bigr)=\\frac{1-(n+1-c)}{1}=-(n-c),\n\\]\n\nwhich are finite, non\u2011zero and different from \\(-1\\) because \\(n-c\\ge 2\\). Hence both lines are sunny.\n\nObserve that after the replacement described below the vertical line \\(x=c+1\\) remains in the family. Consequently every point of the column  \n\n\\[\nC_c=\\{(c,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\}\n\\]\n\nis covered:\n\n* the two endpoints \\((c,1)\\) and \\((c,n+1-c)\\) lie on \\(\\ell_c^{(1)}\\) and \\(\\ell_c^{(2)}\\) respectively;\n* all other points of the column have the same \\(x\\)-coordinate \\(c\\) but different \\(y\\); they are not on the new sunny lines, but they are on the unchanged vertical line \\(x=c+1\\) after a shift of the index: the point \\((c,b)\\) with \\(b\\neq 1,n+1-c\\) coincides with the point \\((c+1,b-1)\\) of the next column, and that point is on the vertical line \\(x=c+1\\) which is still present.  \n\nThus the covering property is preserved.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Constructing families with any prescribed number of sunny lines  \n\n#### Lemma\u202f2  \nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) that contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nStart from the base family \\(\\mathcal L_{\\text{base}}\\) of Lemma\u202f1 (which has \\(k=0\\) sunny lines).  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nt=\\bigl\\lfloor k/2\\bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(c=2,3,\\dots ,t+1\\) (note that \\(t+1\\le n-2\\) because \\(k\\le n-1\\)) replace the vertical line \\(x=c\\) by the **pair** \\(\\{\\ell_c^{(1)},\\ell_c^{(2)}\\}\\) defined in (2). By the discussion in \u00a73 the covering of \\(S_n\\) is unchanged, the total number of lines stays \\(n\\), and the number of sunny lines increases by two. After these \\(t\\) replacements we have \\(2t\\) sunny lines.\n\nIf \\(k\\) is odd we need one more sunny line. Consider the next column \\(c=t+2\\) (still satisfying \\(c\\le n-2\\)). Replace the vertical line \\(x=c\\) **only** by the single sunny line \\(\\ell_c^{(1)}\\). The point \\((c,1)\\) is now on \\(\\ell_c^{(1)}\\); the point \\((c,n+1-c)\\) remains covered because it lies on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) that has never been removed. All other points of this column are covered as before by the vertical line \\(x=c+1\\). Hence the number of sunny lines increases by exactly one, reaching \\(k\\).\n\nAll lines introduced are distinct: each \\(\\ell_c^{(1)}\\) and \\(\\ell_c^{(2)}\\) has a unique pair of integer endpoints, and none coincides with any other line of the construction. Consequently we obtain a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) with precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  An unavoidable non\u2011sunny line  \n\n#### Lemma\u202f3  \nIn any covering of \\(S_n\\) by \\(n\\) distinct lines, at least one line is non\u2011sunny. Consequently the number of sunny lines satisfies \\(k\\le n-1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the set  \n\n\\[\nR=\\{(i,1)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\nthe \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\) lying on the bottom side of the triangle.  \nIf none of the \\(n\\) lines is horizontal, then each line can contain at most one point of \\(R\\) (two distinct points of \\(R\\) have the same \\(y\\)-coordinate, forcing a horizontal line). Hence at least \\(n\\) distinct lines would be needed, contradicting the hypothesis. Therefore a horizontal line is present; the only horizontal line that can contain points of \\(R\\) is \\(y=1\\), which is non\u2011sunny.\n\nThus any admissible family contains at least one non\u2011sunny line, so the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n-1\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Conclusion  \n\nLemma\u202f2 shows that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) a covering with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists. Lemma\u202f3 proves that no covering can have more than \\(n-1\\) sunny lines. Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-1\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\u220e"

