et  \n\n\\[\nt=\\bigl\\lfloor k/2\\bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(c=2,3,\\dots ,t+1\\) (note that \\(t+1\\le n-2\\) because \\(k\\le n-1\\)) perform the **pair** replacement described in §3, i.e. replace the vertical line \\(x=c\\) by the two sunny lines \\(\\ell_c^{(1)}\\) and \\(\\ell_c^{(2)}\\). After these \\(t\\) steps we have added \\(2t\\) sunny lines.\n\nIf \\(k\\) is even we are done.  \nIf \\(k\\) is odd, choose the next column index \\(c=t+2\\) (still \\(\\le n-2\\)) and perform the **single** replacement of §4: replace the vertical line \\(x=c\\) by the single sunny line \\(\\ell_c^{(2)}\\) while keeping the horizontal line \\(y=1\\). This adds one further sunny line, bringing the total to \\(2t+1=k\\).\n\nAll introduced lines are distinct: each \\(\\ell_c^{(1)}\\) and \\(\\ell_c^{(2)}\\) has a unique pair of integer endpoints, and none coincides with any other line of the construction or with the original vertical/horizontal lines that remain. The total number of lines stays \\(n\\) (each replacement removes exactly one vertical line and inserts one or two new lines, compensating the count). Hence we obtain a covering with precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Necessity of at least one non‑sunny line  \n\n#### Lemma 3  \nIn any covering of \\(S_n\\) by \\(n\\) distinct lines, at least one line is non‑sunny. Consequently the number of sunny lines satisfies \\(k\\le n-1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the set  \n\n\\[\nR=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\}\\subset S_n .\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(R\\) lie on the same horizontal line \\(y=1\\). If a line contains two distinct points of \\(R\\) it must be horizontal, hence non‑sunny. Suppose, for contradiction, that **all** \\(n\\) lines of the covering are sunny. Then no line can contain two points of \\(R\\); consequently each line of the covering contains at most one point of \\(R\\). Because there are exactly \\(n\\) points in \\(R\\), the covering would have to consist of precisely the \\(n\\) lines each passing through a distinct point \\((a,1)\\).\n\nTake the point \\((1,n)\\in S_n\\) (since \\(1+n=n+1\\)). This point does **not** lie on any line through \\((a,1)\\) with \\(a\\ge 2\\) because such a line has finite non‑zero slope and cannot pass through \\((1,n)\\). It also does not lie on the line through \\((1,1)\\) because that line is vertical \\(x=1\\), which does not contain \\((1,n)\\) for \\(n\\ge 2\\). Hence \\((1,n)\\) would be uncovered, contradicting the assumption that the \\(n\\) lines cover all of \\(S_n\\).\n\nTherefore at least one line in any covering must be non‑sunny, and the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n-1\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Final answer  \n\nLemma 2 shows that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) a covering with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists. Lemma 3 proves that no covering can have more than \\(n-1\\) sunny lines. Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-1\\}} .\n\\] \n\n∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
