ain at most one point of \\(\\mathcal T_n\\) for each distinct integer value of \\(\\Delta\\).\n\nBecause the points of \\(\\mathcal T_n\\) have integer coordinates, the set\n\\[\n\\{\\Delta_{a,b}=b-m a\\mid (a,b)\\in\\mathcal T_n\\}\n\\]\ncontains at least \\(n\\) distinct integers (indeed, varying \\(a\\) from \\(1\\) to \\(n\\) while taking \\(b=n+1-a\\) yields the values \\(\\Delta_{a,n+1-a}=n+1-a-m a\\), which are pairwise distinct).  \nHence a single sunny line of slope \\(m\\) can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal T_n\\).\n\nWith only \\(n\\) lines, the total number of points that can be covered is therefore at most \\(n\\cdot n=n^{2}\\).  \nHowever,\n\\[\n|\\mathcal T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>n^{2}\\qquad\\text{for every }n\\ge 3,\n\\]\na contradiction.  \nThus no configuration with all \\(n\\) lines sunny can satisfy the covering condition.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
