us at most \\(n\\) points lie on the line.\n\n* horizontal: analogous, at most \\(n\\) points.\n\n* of slope \\(-1\\): equation \\(x+y=c\\) with integer \\(c\\).  The admissible points satisfy \\(1\\le a\\le c-1\\) and \\(c\\le n+1\\); consequently there are at most \\(c-1\\le n\\) points. ∎\n\n\n\nTherefore the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines together can cover at most \\((n-k)n\\) points of \\(S\\).\n\n---\n\nPutting the two lemmas together, the total number of points that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\nk + (n-k)n . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSince every point of \\(S\\) must be covered, (2) must be at least \\(|S|\\) given by (1).  Hence  \n\n\\[\nk + (n-k)n \\;\\ge\\; \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nRearranging,\n\n\\[\nk(n-1) \\;\\le\\; \\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nk \\;\\le\\; \\frac{n}{2}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(k\\) is an integer we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k\\le \\bigl\\lfloor \\tfrac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\,}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 3.  Construction attaining every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor \\tfrac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).\n\n1. **Sunny lines.**  \n   For each \\(i\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,k\\}\\) consider the point  \n\n   \\[\n   P_i=(i,\\;n+1-i)\\in S .\n   \\]\n\n   The points \\(P_i\\) lie on the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) and are distinct because the first coordinates are distinct.  \n   Through each \\(P_i\\) draw the line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\; y-(n+1-i)=2\\bigl(x-i\\bigr) .\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of every \\(L_i\\) is \\(2\\), which is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); consequently each \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  \n   By Lemma 2.1 a sunny line can meet \\(S\\) in at most one point, and indeed each \\(L_i\\) meets \\(S\\) exactly at \\(P_i\\).\n\n2. **Non‑sunny lines.**  \n   The remaining points of \\(S\\) are those with \\(a+b\\le n\\).  For any such point we have \\(a\\le n-k\\) because if \\(a>n-k\\) then \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\) and, using \\