y line.\n\nSince the three possibilities exhaust all ways two points can share a vertical, horizontal or slope \\(-1\\) line, none can occur. ∎\n\n\n\nHence the \\(k\\) sunny lines together cover at most \\(k\\) points of \\(S\\).\n\n---\n\n**Lemma 2.2.** *A non‑sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S\\).*\n\n*Proof.*  \nA non‑sunny line is either  \n\n* vertical: \\(x=c\\) with integer \\(c\\).  The condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(1\\le c\\le n\\), and for a fixed \\(c\\) the admissible \\(b\\)’s are \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\); thus at most \\(n\\) points lie on the line.\n\n* horizontal: analogous, at most \\(n\\) points.\n\n* of slope \\(-1\\): equation \\(x+y=c\\) with integer \\(c\\).  The admissible points satisfy \\(1\\le a\\le c-1\\) and \\(c\\le n+1\\); consequently there are at most \\(c-1\\le n\\) points. ∎\n\n\n\nTherefore the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines together can cover at most \\((n-k)n\\) points of \\(S\\).\n\n---\n\nPutting the two lemmas together, the total number of points that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\nk + (n-k)n . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSince every point of \\(S\\) must be covered, (2) must be at least \\(|S|\\) given by (1).  Hence  \n\n\\[\nk + (n-k)n \\;\\ge\\; \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nRearranging,\n\n\\[\nk(n-1) \\;\\le\\; \\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nk \\;\\le\\; \\frac{n}{2}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(k\\) is an integer we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k\\le \\bigl\\lfloor \\tfrac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\,}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 3.  Construction attaining every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor \\tfrac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).\n\n1. **Sunny lines.**  \n   For each \\(i\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,k\\}\\) consider the point  \n\n   \\[\n   P_i=(i,\\;n+1-i)\\in S .\n   \\]\n\n   The points \\(P_i\\) lie on the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) and are distinct because the first coordinates are distinct.  \n   Through each \\(P_i\\) draw the line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\; y-(n+1-i)=2\\bigl(x-i\\bigr) .\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of every \\(L_i\\) is \\(2\\), which is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); consequently each \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  \n   By Lemma 2.1 a sunny line can meet \\(S\\) in at most one point, and indeed each \\(L_i\\) meets \\(S\\) exactly at \\(P_i\\).\n\n2. **Non‑sunny lines.**  \n   The remaining points of \\(S\\) are those with \\(a+b\\le n\\).  For any such point we have \\(a\\le n-k\\) because if \\(a>n-k\\) then \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\) and, using \\(a+b\\le n\\), we would get \\(b\\le k-1\\); but then \\(a+b\\le (n-k+1)+(k-1)=n\\) forces \\(a\\le n-k\\), a contradiction.  Hence every point with \\(a+b\\le n\\) satisfies \\(1\\le a\\le n-k\\).\n\n   Therefore all these points are covered by the vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   V_j:\\; x=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n   \\]\n\n   Each \\(V_j\\) is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non‑sunny.\n\n3. **Counting lines.**  \n   We have produced exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\{L_i\\}\\) and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines \\(\\{V_j\\}\\); altogether \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct lines.  \n   By construction every point of \\(S\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) a suitable family of \\(n\\) lines exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 4.  Conclusion  \n\nThe upper bound (3) shows that no configuration can contain more than \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) sunny lines, and the explicit construction of §3 shows that every integer between \\(0\\) and \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) is attainable.  Hence the complete set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\; \\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\bigl\\lfloor \\tfrac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\}\\; } .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
