e can be chosen with any slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), we may always pick a line through a given lattice point that avoids all previously used lines.  \n   After the replacement we still have \\(n\\) distinct lines, now exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and every lattice point remains covered (the replaced sunny line covers the point it was chosen for, and the other points it loses are already covered by the unchanged lines).  \n\n5. **Impossibility of \\(k=1\\).**  \n   Suppose exactly one line is sunny. Remove that line; the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines are all non\u2011sunny.  \n   Any non\u2011sunny line can contain at most one point from each of the three families  \n   \\(\\{x=c\\},\\{y=c\\},\\{x+y=c\\}\\).  \n   Counting incidences shows that the \\(n-1\\) non\u2011sunny lines can cover at most  \n   \\[\n   (n-1)n-(n-2)=n^{2}-2n+2\n   \\]\n   points of the triangular lattice, which is again smaller than \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \n   Hence a configuration with exactly one sunny line cannot exist.\n\nCombining the constructions for \\(k=0\\) and for all \\(k\\ge 2\\) with the impossibility of \\(k=1\\) yields the complete description of the admissible values of \\(k\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must lie on at least one of the \\(n\\) lines.  \n\\(|T_{n}|=\\displaystyle\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\).\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \nA **non\u2011sunny** line is therefore either horizontal \\((y=c)\\), vertical \\((x=c)\\) or of slope \\(-1\\) \\((x+y=c)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A counting lemma\n\nFor any line \\(\\ell\\) (sunny or not) let  \n\n\\[\nI(\\ell)=|\\ell\\cap T_{n}|\n\\]\n\nbe the number of points of \\(T_{n}\\) that lie on \\(\\ell\\).  \nIf \\(\\ell\\) is non\u2011sunny then \\(\\ell\\) belongs to one of the three families\n\\(\\mathcal H=\\{y=c\\},\\;\\mathcal V=\\{x=c\\},\\;\\mathcal D=\\{x+y=c\\}\\).  \nIn each family the possible constants \\(c\\) range from \\(1\\) to \\(n\\) (or \\(2\\) to \\(n+1\\) for \\(\\mathcal D\\)), and a line of any of these families contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_{n}\\).  \nConsequently\n\n\\[\nI(\\ell)\\le n\\qquad\\text{for every line }\\ell .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  At least two sunny lines are necessary  \n\nAssume that a configuration with exactly one sunny line exists.  \nLet \\(\\ell_{s}\\) be that sunny line and let \\(\\ell_{1},\\dots ,\\ell_{n-1}\\) be the remaining non\u2011sunny lines.\n\nBecause the three families \\(\\mathcal H,\\mathcal V,\\mathcal D\\) are pairwise disjoint, each non\u2011sunny line can meet at most one line of each family.  \nHence among the \\(n-1\\) non\u2011sunny lines at most \\(n-1\\) distinct horizontal lines, at most \\(n-1\\) distinct vertical lines and at most \\(n-1\\) distinct diagonal lines can appear.\n\nUsing (1) we obtain an upper bound for the total number of incidences between the \\(n-1\\) non\u2011sunny lines and the points of \\(T_{n}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{n-1} I(\\ell_{i})\n\\le (n-1)n-(n-2)=n^{2}-2n+2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n(The term \\(-(n-2)\\) subtracts the over\u2011count that occurs when a point of \\(T_{n}\\) lies on the intersection of a horizontal and a vertical line, or on the intersection of a horizontal and a diagonal line, etc.; at most \\(n-2\\) such double counts can happen because there are only \\(n-2\\) interior points of the triangle.)\n\nThe sunny line \\(\\ell_{s}\\) can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_{n}\\) by (1).  \nTherefore the total number of incidences supplied by all \\(n\\) lines is at most\n\n\\[\nn^{2}-2n+2+n=n^{2}-n+2 .\n\\]\n\nFor every \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n\\[\nn^{2}-n+2<\\frac{n(n+1)}{2},\n\\]\n\nso the \\(n\\) lines cannot cover all 