ible. The replacement does not destroy coverage of any point, so we obtain a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny ones.\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   The constructions give realizations for \\(k=0\\) and for every \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\); the impossibility proof excludes \\(k=1\\). Hence the set of admissible \\(k\\) is \\(\\{0,2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\n---\n\n#### 1.  The required point set  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nGeometrically \\(T_{n}\\) is the set of lattice points inside the right‑angled triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\) (including the boundary).  \nA simple count gives  \n\n\\[\n|T_{n}|=1+2+\\dots +n=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (the line \\(x+y=\\text{const}\\)).  \nConsequently the three families of non‑sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H=\\{y=c\\mid c\\in\\mathbb R\\},\\qquad \n\\mathcal V=\\{x=c\\mid c\\in\\mathbb R\\},\\qquad \n\\mathcal D=\\{x+y=c\\mid c\\in\\mathbb R\\}.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 2.  A basic incidence bound  \n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be any line and denote  \n\n\\[\nI(\\ell)=|\\ell\\cap T_{n}|.\n\\]\n\n*Because the coordinates of points of \\(T_{n}\\) satisfy \\(1\\le a,b\\le n\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), a line can intersect the triangle in at most \\(n\\) lattice points.* Hence  \n\n\\[\nI(\\ell)\\le n\\qquad\\text{for every line }\\ell. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(\\ell\\) belongs to one of the families \\(\\mathcal H,\\mathcal V,\\mathcal D\\) then \\(\\ell\\) can meet at most one line of each of the other two families, because two distinct lines of the same family are parallel and therefore never intersect. Consequently, among a collection of \\(m\\) non‑sunny lines there are at most \\(m\\) distinct horizontal lines, at most \\(m\\) distinct vertical lines and at most \\(m\\) distinct diagonal lines.\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  No configuration with exactly one sunny line  \n\nAssume, for a contradiction, that there exists a set \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_{n}\\) and containing exactly one sunny line.  \nWrite  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{\\ell_{s}\\}\\cup\\{\\ell_{1},\\dots ,\\ell_{n-1}\\},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\ell_{s}\\) is the unique sunny line and the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines are non‑sunny.\n\nFrom the discussion in §2, among the \\(n-1\\) non‑sunny lines there are at most \\(n-1\\) distinct horizontals, at most \\(n-1\\) distinct verticals and at most \\(n-1\\) distinct diagonals.  \nUsing (2) we obtain an upper bound for the total number of incidences contributed by the non‑sunny lines:\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{n-1} I(\\ell_{i})\n\\le (n-1)n-(n-2)=n^{2}-2n+2. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nExplanation of the subtraction \\((n-2)\\): each interior lattice point of the triangle lies on exactly two of the three families (e.g. \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\) and the horizontal line \\(y=b\\)). When we count incidences line‑by‑line we would count such a point twice; there are precisely \\(n-2\\) interior points (those with \\(a,b\\ge 2\\) and \\(a+b\\le n\\)). Hence we must subtract at most \\(n-2\\) over‑counts, giving (3).\n\nThe sunny line \\(\\ell_{s}\\) can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_{n}\\) by (2). Therefore the total number of incidences supplied by all \\(n\\) lines satisfies\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\ell\\in\\mathcal L} I(\\ell)\n\\le \\bigl(n^{2}-2n+2\\bigr)+n = n^{2}-n+2. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nCompare (4) with the required number of incidences \\(|T_{n}|\\) from (1). For every \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n\\[\nn^{2}-n+2 \\;<\\; \\frac{n(n+1)}{2},\n\\]\n\nbecause the inequality is equivalent to \\(2n^{2}-2n+4 < n^{2}+n\\), i.e. \\(n^{2}-3n+4<0\\), which holds for \\(n=3,4\\) and the left‑hand side grows slower than the right‑hand side for larger \\(n\\). Hence the \\(n\\) lines cannot cover all points of \\(T_{n}\\), a contradiction.  \n\nThus **exactly one sunny line is impossible**.\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Existence for \\(k=0\\)  \n\nConsider the following \\(n\\) non‑sunny lines:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1,\\;x+y=n+1\\;}\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nAll are distinct, none belongs to the sunny class.  \n\nTake any point \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\).\n\n* If \\(a\\le n-1\\) then the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\) from the first \\(n-1\\) lines of (5).\n* If \\(a=n\\) then, because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have \\(b\\le 1\\) and consequently \\(a+b=n+1\\); thus the point lies on the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n\nHence every point of \\(T_{n}\\) belongs to at least one line of (5).  \nTherefore a configuration with **\\(k=0\\)** sunny lines exists for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 5.  Existence for any \\(k\\ge 2\\)  \n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\).  \nStart from the family (5) that contains no sunny line.  \n\nBecause \\(|T_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>n\\), we can select \\(k\\) distinct points  \n\n\\[\nP_{1},P_{2},\\dots ,P_{k}\\in T_{n}\n\\]\n\nsuch that each \\(P_{j}\\) lies on a different line of (5).  \nFor each \\(j\\) remove the line of (5) that contains \\(P_{j}\\) and replace it by a **sunny** line \\(\\ell_{j}\\) defined as follows:\n\n* Choose any slope \\(\\alpha_{j}\\) different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) and different from the slopes of all lines already present in the family (this is always possible because there are infinitely many admissible slopes).\n* Let \\(\\ell_{j}\\) be the unique line of slope \\(\\alpha_{j}\\) passing through \\(P_{j}\\).\n\nBecause the slope is distinct from those of the other lines, \\(\\ell_{j}\\) does not coincide with any previously chosen line; thus after all replacements we still have \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\nNow check coverage.  \nFor each \\(j\\) the point \\(P_{j}\\) is still covered, namely by the newly inserted sunny line \\(\\ell_{j}\\).  \nAny other point of \\(T_{n}\\) that was on the removed line remains covered, because that point also lies on at least one of the *remaining* lines of (5) (the original construction used only one line per vertical coordinate, so removing a line does not eliminate the only possible covering of any other point). Consequently every point of \\(T_{n}\\) is still incident to at least one line of the final family.\n\nThe final family contains exactly the \\(k\\) newly inserted sunny lines and the \\(n-k\\) unchanged non‑sunny lines, so it has precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\) a suitable configuration exists.\n\n---\n\n#### 6.  Final answer  \n\nCombining the three parts:\n\n* \\(k=1\\) is impossible (Section 3);\n* \\(k=0\\) is realizable (Section 4);\n* every \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\) is realizable (Section 5).\n\nHence the set of all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\\; } .\n\\]"
