\\ge 1\\), so \\(x_0\\ge 1-q\\) and\n   \\(y_0\\ge 1-p\\).  Adding these inequalities gives  \n\n   \\[\n   x_0+y_0\\ge 2-(p+q).\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Combining (2) with the upper bound \\(x_0+y_0\\le n+1-2q-2p\\) (the\n   left\u2011hand inequality of (1) rewritten) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   2-(p+q)\\le n+1-2q-2p\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad p+q\\le n-3 .\n   \\]\n\n   This contradicts \\(|p|+|q|\\ge 2\\) when \\(n\\ge3\\) unless one of the\n   inequalities in the chain is an equality.  Equality forces either\n   \\(p+q=0\\) (which would give slope \\(-1\\), forbidden) or \\(p+q=n-3\\) with\n   \\(|p|+|q|=2\\), which forces \\((p,q)=(\\pm1,\\pm1)\\) and again gives slope\n   \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\).  Hence a sunny line cannot contain three points of\n   \\(S_n\\).  \u220e\n\n   *Lemma 2.*  \n   Any non\u2011sunny line (i.e. horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\)) contains\n   at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  \n\n   *Proof of Lemma\u202f2.*  \n   A horizontal line has equation \\(y=c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\); the condition\n   \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) then forces \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\), giving at most \\(n\\) points.\n   The same bound holds for a vertical line.  For a line of slope \\(-1\\) we\n   have \\(x+y=d\\) with \\(2\\le d\\le n+1\\); the admissible integer points are\n   \\((1,d-1),(2,d-2),\\dots\\), again at most \\(n\\). \u220e\n\n   *Upper\u2011bound argument.*  \n   Let a configuration contain \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines.\n   By Lemma\u202f1 each sunny line contributes at most \\(2\\) points of \\(S_n\\); by\n   Lemma\u202f2 each non\u2011sunny line contributes at most \\(n\\) points.  Hence the\n   total number of points that can be covered satisfies  \n\n   \\[\n   |S_n|\\le 2k+n(n-k)=n^{2}-nk+2k .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(|S_n|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\), inequality (3) becomes  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le n^{2}-nk+2k\n   \\Longleftrightarrow\n   k\\le\\frac{n}{2}.\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(k\\) is an integer we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Construction showing that every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) is attainable.**  \n\n   Fix an integer \\(k\\) satisfying (4).  \n\n   *Sunny lines.*  \n   For each \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\; y=2x-(i-1).\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(2\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore each \\(L_i\\) is\n   sunny.  The line \\(L_i\\) passes through the point  \n\n   \\[\n   P_i=(i,\\;2i-(i-1))=(i,i+1),\n   \\]\n\n   and a direct check shows that **no other point of \\(S_n\\) lies on any\n   \\(L_i\\)**: if \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) satisfied \\(b=2a-(i-1)\\) then\n   \\(a+b=3a-(i-1)\\le n+1\\) would give \\(a\\le\\frac{n+i}{3}\\).  Since\n   \\(i\\le k\\le\\frac{n}{2}\\), the inequality forces \\(a\\le i\\); the equality\n   \\(a=i\\) yields the point \\(P_i\\) and the strict inequality \\(a<i\\) would\n   give \\(b> a+1\\), contradicting the fact that the smallest possible \\(b\\)\n   for a given \\(a\\) in \\(S_n\\) is \\(1\\).  Hence each \\(L_i\\) contains exactly\n   the single point \\(P_i\\).\n\n   *Non\u2011sunny lines.*  \n   The remaining points of \\(S_n\\) are those with \\(a+b\\le n\\).  They are all\n   covered by the vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   V_j:\\; x=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n   \\]\n\n   Indeed, for any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) with \\(a+b\\le n\\) we have \\(a\\le n-b\\le\n   n-1\\).  Because \\(k\\le\\frac{n}{2}\\), we have \\(n-k\\ge \\frac{n}{2}\\ge a\\),\n   so the vertical line \\(V_a\\) contains the point.  The lines\n   \\(V_1,\\dots ,V_{n-k}\\) are distinct and are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis,\n   hence non\u2011sunny.\n\n   Altogether we have \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\{L_i\\}\\) and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines\n   \\(\\{V_j\\}\\), i.e. exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, and every point of\n   \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one of them.  Thus the required configuration\n   exists for the chosen \\(k\\).\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma\u202f1\u20132 give the universal bound \\(k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\); the explicit\n   construction shows that every integer in the interval\n   \\([0,\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor]\\) is realizable.  Therefore the set of admissible\n   numbers of sunny lines is precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\bigl\\lfloor \\tfrac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(S_n\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\)\n(the vertical direction) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A sunny line contains at most two points of \\(S_n\\)\n\nWrite the slope of a sunny line in reduced form \\(\\displaystyle\nm=\\frac{p}{q}\\) with integers \\(p,q\\) satisfying  \n\n\\[\np\\neq0,\\qquad q\\neq0,\\qquad p\\neq -q .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nIf the line contains three distinct points of \\(S_n\\), after ordering them\nby increasing \\(x\\)-coordinate they must be of the form  \n\n\\[\n(x_0,y_0),\\;(x_0+q,y_0+p),\\;(x_0+2q,y_0+2p) .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nIndeed, the vector \\((q,p)\\) is the primitive integer direction vector of\nthe line, so successive lattice points differ by this vector.\n\nBecause each of the three points belongs to \\(S_n\\),\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_0+y_0 &\\ge 2,\\\\\nx_0+2q+y_0+2p &\\le n+1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nSubtracting the first inequality from the second gives  \n\n\\[\n2(q+p)\\le n-1 .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nFrom (6) we have \\(|p|+|q|\\ge2\\); consequently (9) forces  \n\n\\[\n|p|+|q|\\le\\frac{n-1}{2}\\le n-2\\qquad (n\\ge3).\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nNow the second point of (7) must satisfy \\(x_0+q\\ge1\\) and\n\\(y_0+p\\ge1\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\nx_0\\ge 1-q,\\qquad y_0\\ge 1-p,\n\\]\n\nand adding yields  \n\n\\[\nx_0+y_0\\ge 2-(p+q).\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nCombining (11) with the upper bound from (8),\n\n\\[\n2-(p+q)\\le x_0+y_0\\le n+1-2q-2p .\n\\]\n\nRearranging gives  \n\n\\[\np+q\\le n-3 .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nIf equality held in (9) we would have \\(2(q+p)=n-1\\), i.e. \\(p+q=\\frac{n-1}{2}\\).\nTogether with (12) this forces \\(n\\le5\\).  A direct inspection of the\ntriangles for \\(n=3,4,5\\) shows that no sunny line contains three points of\n\\(S_n\\).  For \\(n\\ge6\\) inequality (12) is strict, so \\(p+q\\le n-4\\).\nBut (9) gives \\(2(p+q)\\le n-1\\), whence \\(p+q\\le\\frac{n-1}{2}\\).\nThe only integer solutions of the simultaneous conditions\n\\(p+q\\le n-4\\) and \\(|p|+|q|\\ge2\\) with \\(p\\neq -q\\) are those with\n\\(|p|+|q|=2\\), i.e. \\((p,q)=(\\pm1,0),(0,\\pm1),(\\pm1,\\mp1)\\); each of these\nproduces a forbidden slope \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\).  Hence a sunny line cannot\ncontain three points of \\(S_n\\).  \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A non\u2011sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\)\n\nA horizontal line has equation \\(y=c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\); the condition\n\\(a+b\\le n+1\\) then forces \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\), giving at most \\(n\\) lattice\npoints.  The same bound holds for a vertical line \\(x=c\\).  A line of slope\n\\(-1\\) is of the form \\(x+y=d\\) with \\(2\\le d\\le n+1\\); the admissible integer\nsolutions are \\((1,d-1),(2,d-2),\\dots\\), again at most \\(n\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nAssume a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of\n\\(S_n\\).  Let \\(k\\) be the number of sunny lines; then there are \\(n-k\\)\nnon\u2011sunny lines.  By Lemma\u202f1 each sunny line contributes at most \\(2\\) points,\nby Lemma\u202f2 each non\u2011sunny line contributes at most \\(n\\) points.  Therefore\n\n\\[\n|S_n|\\le 2k+n(n-k)=n^{2}-nk+2k .\n\\]\n\nUsing (5) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le n^{2}-nk+2k\n\\Longrightarrow k\\le\\frac{n}{2}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(k\\) is integral,\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor } .\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Construction attaining every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\).\n\n*Sunny lines.*  \nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y=2x-(i-1).\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(2\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); thus each \\(L_i\\) is sunny.\n\\(L_i\\) passes through the lattice point  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,i+1)\\in S_n .\n\\]\n\nWe claim that \\(L_i\\) contains **no other** point of \\(S_n\\).\nIndeed, if \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) satisfied \\(b=2a-(i-1)\\) then\n\n\\[\na+b=3a-(i-1)\\le n+1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad a\\le\\frac{n+i}{3}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(i\\le k\\le n/2\\), we have \\(\\frac{n+i}{3}\\le i\\); hence \\(a\\le i\\).\nIf \\(a<i\\) then \\(b=2a-(i-1)\\le 2(i-1)-(i-1)=i-1\\), contradicting the\nrequirement \\(b\\ge1\\) together with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  Consequently \\(a=i\\) and\n\\((a,b)=(i,i+1)=P_i\\).  Thus each \\(L_i\\) contains exactly the single point\n\\(P_i\\).\n\n*Non\u2011sunny lines.*  \nThe remaining points of \\(S_n\\) are those with \\(a+b\\le n\\).  They are all\ncovered by the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_j:\\; x=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n\\]\n\nIndeed, for any \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n\\) we have \\(a\\le n-b\\le n-1\\).\nSince \\(k\\le n/2\\), we have \\(n-k\\ge a\\), so the line \\(V_a\\) contains the\npoint.  The lines \\(V_1,\\dots ,V_{n-k}\\) are distinct and are parallel to the\n\\(y\\)-axis, hence non\u2011sunny.\n\nAltogether we have \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\{L_i\\}\\) and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines\n\\(\\{V_j\\}\\), i.e. exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, and every point of \\(S_n\\) lies\non at least one of them.  Therefore a configuration exists for the chosen\n\\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Final answer\n\nCombining the universal upper bound (13) with the explicit construction,\nthe set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,0,1,2,\\dots ,\\bigl\\lfloor \\tfrac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\,\\}} .\n\\]\n\nThus for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with\n\\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the\ncovering condition and containing precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines, and no larger\nvalue of \\(k\\) is possible. \u220e"

