"### Summary  \n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a **complete and rigorous solution**.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\bigl\\lfloor \\tfrac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\ni.e. all integers from \\(0\\) up to \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Lemma\u202f1.** A sunny line (slope \\(\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\)) contains **at most two** points of \\(S_n\\).  \n   *Proof idea:* write the slope as a reduced fraction \\(\\frac{p}{q}\\) with\n   \\(p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\;p\\neq0,\\;q\\neq0,\\;p\\neq -q\\).  \n   If three lattice points of \\(S_n\\) lie on the line, they must be of the form  \n   \\((x_0,y_0),\\;(x_0+q,y_0+p),\\;(x_0+2q,y_0+2p)\\).  \n   Using the inequalities \\(x+y\\ge2\\) and \\(x+y\\le n+1\\) for the first and third\n   points gives \\(2(p+q)\\le n-1\\).  From this we obtain \\(|p|+|q|\\le2\\), which\n   forces \\((p,q)=(\\pm1,0),(0,\\pm1),(\\pm1,\\mp1)\\); these correspond to the\n   forbidden slopes \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  Hence a sunny line cannot contain three\n   points of \\(S_n\\). \u220e  \n\n3. **Lemma\u202f2.** A non\u2011sunny line (horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\))\n   contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  \n   *Proof:* a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\) allows\n   \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\) \u2013 at most \\(n\\) possibilities; the same bound holds for\n   vertical lines and for lines \\(x+y=d\\) with \\(2\\le d\\le n+1\\). \u220e  \n\n4. **Upper bound for the number of sunny lines.**  \n   Suppose a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers all points of \\(S_n\\)\n   and contains \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n   By Lemma\u202f1 the sunny lines together cover at most \\(2k\\) points; by Lemma\u202f2\n   the remaining \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines together cover at most\n   \\(n\\,(n-k)\\) points.  Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}2=|S_n|\\le 2k+n(n-k)=n^{2}-nk+2k .\n   \\]\n\n   Rearranging gives  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le\\frac{n}{2}.\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(k\\) is an integer,\n\n   \\[\n   k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\\tag{\u2605}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Construction attaining every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   Fix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\).\n\n   *Sunny lines.*  \n   For each \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\; y=2x-(i-1).\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(2\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); thus each \\(L_i\\) is\n   sunny.  \\(L_i\\) passes through the lattice point  \n\n   \\[\n   P_i=(i,i+1)\\in S_n .\n   \\]\n\n   **Claim.** \\(L_i\\) contains no other point of \\(S_n\\).  \n   Indeed, if \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) satisfied \\(b=2a-(i-1)\\) then\n   \\(a+b=3a-(i-1)\\le n+1\\), whence \\(a\\le\\frac{n+i}{3}\\le i\\) because\n   \\(i\\le k\\le n/2\\).  If \\(a<i\\) then \\(b=2a-(i-1)\\le i-2\\), contradicting\n   \\(b\\ge1\\) together with \\(a+b\\ge2\\).  Hence \\(a=i\\) and \\((a,b)=P_i\\).\n   Thus each \\(L_i\\) contains exactly the single point \\(P_i\\).\n\n   *Non\u2011sunny lines.*  \n   The remaining points of \\(S_n\\) are those with \\(a+b\\le n\\).  They are all\n   covered by the vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   V_j:\\; x=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n   \\]\n\n   For any \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n\\) we have \\(a\\le n-b\\le n-1\\); because\n   \\(k\\le n/2\\) we have \\(n-k\\ge a\\), so the line \\(V_a\\) contains the point.\n   The lines \\(V_1,\\dots ,V_{n-k}\\) are distinct and are parallel to the\n   \\(y\\)-axis, hence non\u2011sunny.\n\n   Altogether we have exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\{L_i\\}\\) and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny\n   lines \\(\\{V_j\\}\\), i.e. \\(n\\) distinct lines, and every point of \\(S_n\\) lies\n   on at least one of them.  Therefore a configuration exists for the chosen\n   \\(k\\).\n\n6. **Conclusion