:\\; y=2x-(2i-1).\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(2\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); consequently each\n   \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  Equation (4) can be rewritten as  \n\n   \\[\n   2x-y=2i-1 .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   **Claim 1.**  For each \\(i\\) the line \\(L_i\\) contains **exactly one**\n   point of \\(S_n\\), namely  \n\n   \\[\n   P_i=(i,i+1).\n   \\]\n\n   *Proof.*  Substituting \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) into (5) gives the Diophantine\n   condition  \n\n   \\[\n   2a-b=2i-1 . \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(a,b\\) are positive integers and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), from (6) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   b=2a-(2i-1)\\ge 1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad a\\ge i .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   On the other hand, using \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and (6),\n\n   \\[\n   a+(2a-(2i-1))\\le n+1\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;3a\\le n+2i .\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(k\\le n/2\\) we have \\(2i\\le n\\); combining this with (8) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   a\\le i .\n   \\]\n\n   Together with (7) we obtain \\(a=i\\), and then (6) gives \\(b=i+1\\).\n   Hence the only lattice point of \\(S_n\\) on \\(L_i\\) is \\((i,i+1)\\). ∎\n\n   *Coverage of all points of \\(S_n\\).*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n   - **Case 1.** \\(a\\le n-k\\).  \n     The vertical line \\(V_a\\) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n   - **Case 2.** \\(a> n-k\\).  \n     From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n     \\[\n     b\\le n+1-a\\le n+1-(n-k+1)=k .\n     \\]\n\n     Thus \\(1\\le b\\le k\\).  Set \\(i=b\\).  Because \\(b=i\\) we have  \n\n     \\[\n     a = n+1-b - (n+1-a-b) = i .\n     \\]\n\n     More directly, using (5) with \\(i=b\\),\n\n     \\[\n     2a-b = 2i-1 \\;\\Longrightarrow\\; a=i .\n     \\]\n\n     Hence \\((a,b)=(i,i+1)=P_i\\), which lies on the sunny line \\(L_i\\).\n\n   In both cases the point belongs to at least one of the constructed lines.\n   The \\(n\\) lines \\(\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_{\\,n-k},L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}\\) are distinct,\n   exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and they cover every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   Inequality (3) shows that no configuration can have more than\n   \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) sunny lines.  The explicit construction above shows\n   that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) is attainable.\n   Therefore the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\bigl\\lfloor \\tfrac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
