-\n\n#### Lemma 2  \nNo line \\(L_s\\) is sunny.\n\n*Proof.* By definition each \\(L_s\\) is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\) (they have the same direction vector \\((1,-1)\\)). A sunny line is required **not** to be parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, **or** the line \\(x+y=0\\). Hence each \\(L_s\\) fails the last condition and is therefore not sunny. \u220e\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma 3  \nThe set \\(\\mathcal{L}\\) covers all required points.\n\n*Proof.* Let \\((a,b)\\) be any point with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\). By Lemma\u202f1, \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_{a+b}\\). Since \\(2\\le a+b\\le n+1\\), the line \\(L_{a+b}\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal{L}\\). Hence \\((a,b)\\) is on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal{L}\\). \u220e\n\n---\n\n#### Conclusion  \n\nThe collection \\(\\mathcal{L}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, satisfies the covering condition for all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), and contains **zero** sunny lines (by Lemma\u202f2). Therefore the integer  \n\n\\[\nk=0\n\\]\n\nis realizable for every admissible \\(n\\).\n\n---\n\n*The remaining values of \\(k\\) (i.e. \\(k\\ge 1\\)) have not been settled in this work.*"
