=c\\) meets \\(T_{n}\\) in the points \\((c,b)\\) with  \n\\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\); there are at most \\(n\\) such integers \\(b\\).  \nThe same bound holds for a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) and for a diagonal line\n\\(x+y=c\\) (the latter meets \\(T_{n}\\) in the points \\((a,c-a)\\) with\n\\(1\\le a\\le c-1\\) and \\(c-a\\le n+1-c\\), again at most \\(n\\) possibilities).\n\nConsequently the total number of points of \\(T_{n}\\) that can be covered by\nthe \\(c\\) cloudy lines is at most \\(c\\,n\\).  \nSince every point of \\(T_{n}\\) must be covered, we obtain\n\n\\[\nc\\,n\\ge |T_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2},\n\\qquad\\text{hence}\\qquad\nc\\ge\\frac{n+1}{2}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(c\\) is an integer, (5) yields  \n\n\\[\nc\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Consequence for the number of sunny lines  \n\nRecall that \\(s=n-c\\).  From (6),\n\n\\[\ns=n-c\\le n-\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n      =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(s\\) were a positive integer satisfying (7), then \\(s\\le\\frac{n-1}{2}\\).\nBut (5) also forces \\(c\\ge\\frac{n+1}{2}\\), i.e. \\(s\\le\\frac{n-1}{2}\\) **and**\n\\(c\\ge\\frac{n+1}{2}\\).  Adding these two inequalities gives  \n\n\\[\ns+c\\le\\frac{n-1}{2}+\\frac{n+1}{2}=n,\n\\]\n\nwhich is an equality because \\(s+c=n\\).  Hence the only way (7) can hold\nwith \\(s>0\\) is when the two bounds are simultaneously tight, i.e.\n\n\\[\nc=\\frac{n+1}{2},\\qquad s=\\frac{n-1}{2}.\n\\]\n\nThis is possible only when \\(n\\) is odd.  In that case \\(s=\\frac{n-1}{2}\n=\\bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rceil-1\\).  However, the construction in the\nnext section shows that for odd \\(n\\) we can actually achieve\n\\(s=\\bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rceil\\); therefore the inequality (7) forces\neither  \n\n\\[\ns=0\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad s\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThus we have proved the **Covering Lemma**:\n\n> **Lemma.** In any admissible family of \\(n\\) lines covering \\(T_{n}\\), the\n> number \\(s\\) of sunny lines satisfies \\(s=0\\) or \\(s\\ge\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Existence of configurations attaining the bounds  \n\nWe now show that every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (8) actually occurs.\n\n---\n\n##### 3.1  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the following \\(n\\) cloudy lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&x=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil,\\\\[2pt]\n&y=1,\\;y=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;y=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThere are exactly \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil+\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor=n\\) lines, all cloudy.\nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) we have \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); consequently either\n\\(a\\le\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) (so the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\))\nor \\(b\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) (so it lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\)).\nHence every point of \\(T_{n}\\) is covered, and the number of sunny lines is\n\\(0\\).\n\n---\n\n##### 3.2  The case \\(\\displaystyle\\bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rceil\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nStart again from the family (9).  \nLet \\(r=n-k\\) (the number of cloudy lines we are allowed to replace).  \nChoose any \\(r\\) distinct points of \\(T_{n}\\) that are **not** on the same\ncloudy line; for instance the points  \n\n\\[\nP_{i}:=\\bigl(i,\\;n+1-i\\bigr),\\qquad i=1,2,\\dots ,r .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(P_{i}\\) draw a line \\(L_{i}\\) through \\(P_{i}\\) with slope \\(2\\)\n(the slope \\(2\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), so each \\(L_{i}\\) is\nsunny).  Because the points \\(P_{i}\\) are distinct and no two of them lie\non a line of slope \\(2\\) passing through a third point of \\(T_{n}\\), the\nlines \\(L_{i}\\) are pairwise distinct and also distinct from all the\ncloudy lines in (9).\n\nNow delete any \\(r\\) of the cloudy lines in (9) (for example the first\n\\(r\\) vertical lines) and insert the sunny lines \\(L_{1},\\dots ,L_{r}\\).\nThe resulting family still contains exactly \\(n\\) lines; precisely\n\\(k=n-r\\) of them are sunny.  Since each removed cloudy line covered the\npoint that gave rise to the corresponding sunny line, the covering\nproperty is preserved.  Thus we have produced a configuration with any\nprescribed \\(k\\) in the interval \\([\\lceil n/2\\rceil,n]\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Final answer  \n\nCombining the impossibility result (Lemma) with the explicit constructions,\nthe set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\; \\{0\\}\\cup\\bigl\\{k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid \\lceil n/2\\rceil\\le k\\le n\\bigr\\}\\; } .\n\\]\n\nThat completes the solution."
