unny.  \n     To obtain a sunny line we rotate each of these lines by a tiny angle \\(\\varepsilon\\) (the same \\(\\varepsilon\\) for all of them) so that the new line still passes through the two extreme points \\((1,s-1)\\) and \\((s-1,1)\\) of the diagonal but its slope becomes \\(-1+\\varepsilon\\).  \n\n     Concretely, fix a rational number \\(\\varepsilon\\) with  \n\n     \\[\n     0<\\varepsilon<\\frac{1}{2n}\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad \\varepsilon\\notin\\mathbb Q .\n     \\]\n\n     For each \\(s\\in\\{t+2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) define  \n\n     \\[\n     S_{s}:\\; y = (-1+\\varepsilon)x + (s-\\varepsilon).\n     \\]\n\n     *The line \\(S_s\\) passes through \\((1,s-1)\\) and \\((s-1,1)\\):*  \n\n     \\[\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     y\\big|_{(1,s-1)} &= (-1+\\varepsilon)\\cdot1 + (s-\\varepsilon)=s-1,\\\\\n     y\\big|_{(s-1,1)} &= (-1+\\varepsilon)(s-1)+(s-\\varepsilon)=1 .\n     \\end{aligned}\n     \\]\n\n     Hence every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=s\\) lies on the line segment joining those two extreme points, and because the line is straight, **all** points of the diagonal \\(D_s\\) belong to \\(S_s\\).  \n\n     The slope \\(-1+\\varepsilon\\) is different from \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) and is finite, therefore each \\(S_s\\) is sunny.  \n\n     Distinct values of \\(s\\) give distinct intercepts, so the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\{S_{t+2},\\dots ,S_{n+1}\\}\\) are pairwise different.\n\n4. **Verification.**  \n\n   *All required points are covered.*  \n   - If \\((a,b)\\in D_s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le t+1\\) then \\((a,b)\\in L_s\\) (Step\u202f1).  \n   - If \\((a,b)\\in D_s\\) with \\(t+2\\le s\\le n+1\\) then \\((a,b)\\in S_s\\) (Step\u202f2).  \n\n   Hence every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) lies on at least one of the \\(n\\) constructed lines.\n\n   *Exactly \\(k\\) lines are sunny.*  \n   The construction uses \\(t=n+1-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines \\(L_s\\) and \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(S_s\\); all are distinct, so the total number of lines is \\(t+k=n\\) and precisely \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   For each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) the above procedure yields a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n   Conversely, any admissible configuration contains at most \\(n\\) lines, so \\(k\\) cannot exceed \\(n\\); obviously \\(k\\ge 0\\).  \n   Therefore the set of all possible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n      =\\bigcup_{s=2}^{n+1} D_s ,\\qquad \nD_s:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b=s\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (Non\u2011sunny diagonal lines).\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) the line  \n\n\\[\nL_s:\\;x+y=s\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly the diagonal \\(D_s\\).  \nSince its direction vector is \\((1,-1)\\), \\(L_s\\) is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); consequently \\(L_s\\) is **not** sunny.\n\n*Proof.* Substituting \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=s\\) gives \\(a+b=s\\), so \\((a,b)\\in L_s\\). Conversely, any point of \\(L_s\\) satisfies \\(x+y=s\\) and therefore belongs to \\(D_s\\). The parallelism statement follows from the slope \\(-1\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (A sunny line covering a whole diagonal).\n\nFix a rational number \\(\\varepsilon\\) such that  \n\n\\[\n0<\\varepsilon<\\frac{1}{2n},\\qquad \\varepsilon\\notin\\mathbb Q .\n\\]\n\nFor any integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_s:\\; y = (-1+\\varepsilon)x + (s-\\varepsilon).\n\\]\n\nThen  \n\n* (i) \\(S_s\\) passes through the two extreme points \\((1,s-1)\\) and \\((s-1,1)\\) of the diagonal \\(D_s\\); consequently every point of \\(D_s\\) lies on \\(S_s\\).  \n* (ii) The slope \\(-1+\\varepsilon\\) is different from \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) and is finite; therefore \\(S_s\\) is sunny.  \n* (iii) If \\(s\\neq s'\\) then \\(S_s\\neq S_{s'}\\) because the constant term \\(s-\\varepsilon\\) differs.\n\n*Proof.* Direct substitution shows that both \\((1,s-1)\\) and \\((s-1,1)\\) satisfy the equation of \\(S_s\\). Since a line is uniquely determined by two distinct points, the whole diagonal, which is the set of integer points on the segment joining those two extremes, is contained in \\(S_s\\). The slope condition gives (ii). Distinct intercepts give (iii). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction for a prescribed \\(k\\).\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and put  \n\n\\[\nt:=n+1-k\\qquad (1\\le t\\le n+1).\n\\]\n\n**(a) Non\u2011sunny part.**  \nTake the \\(t-1\\) lines  \n\n\\[\nL_2,\\;L_3,\\dots ,L_{t}\\quad\\text{(i.e. }x+y=s\\text{ for }2\\le s\\le t\\text{)} .\n\\]\n\nBy Lemma\u202f1 each diagonal \\(D_s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le t\\) is covered, and all these lines are non\u2011sunny.\n\n**(b) Sunny part.**  \nFor each remaining diagonal \\(D_s\\) with \\(s\\in\\{t+1,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) (there are exactly \\(k\\) such values) take the sunny line \\(S_s\\) defined in Lemma\u202f2.\n\nThus we obtain the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L:=\\{L_2,\\dots ,L_{t}\\}\\cup\\{S_{t+1},\\dots ,S_{n+1}\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(\\mathcal L\\) is \\((t-1)+k=n\\).  \nAll lines are distinct because the \\(L\\)\u2019s have slope \\(-1\\) whereas the \\(S\\)\u2019s have slope \\(-1+\\varepsilon\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3 (Coverage).\n\nEvery point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in D_s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le t\\) then \\((a,b)\\in L_s\\) by Lemma\u202f1.  \nIf \\(s\\ge t+1\\) then \\((a